  $ \| B_c - B_(*) \| lt.eq frac(C, W sqrt(B_(*))) . $
  On this excess or missing interval the counting density obeys $d lt.eq C B_(*)$,
    by (6.12) and its defining convolution, and $\| epsilon.alt \( x \) \| lt.eq C thin \| \| x \| -
    B_(*) #scale(x: 120%, y: 120%)[\|]$. Therefore replacing the counting interval by $\( - B_(*) \, B_(*) \)$ in
    the dressed-energy integral costs at most
  $ C W B_(*) \| B_c - B_(*) \|^2 lt.eq C / W . $
